Platform: two-socket Xeon Platinum 8480+ (Sapphire Rapids) with its built-in
accelerators set up: one DSA and one IAA work queue configured (accel-config) on
socket 0, a second DSA/IAA pair without queues on socket 1, a QAT 4xxx PF with 4
SR-IOV VFs on socket 0, vfio-pci loaded, AMX, RDT allocation, SNC off (two NUMA
nodes), the On Demand (SDSi) interface per socket and IMC CAS-count events.

Sources (kernel paths are Linux v6.18 (torvalds/linux tag v6.18)):
- DSA 0x0b25 / IAA 0x0cfe, driver idxd, dsa<n>/iax<n> on the dsa bus, work queues
  <dev>/wq<dev>.<n> and /dev/dsa|iax/wq<dev>.<n>: include/linux/pci_ids.h,
  drivers/dma/idxd/init.c, drivers/dma/idxd/cdev.c ("%s/wq%u.%u"),
  Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-driver-dma-idxd.
- /dev/char/<major>:<minor> -> ../dsa/wq0.0: udev's char-device links, which the
  DSA/IAA plugins require; major 375 as in intel-device-plugins v0.37.1
  pkg/idxd/plugin_test.go (dsaMajor).
- QAT PF 0x4940 bound to "4xxx", VFs 0x4941: drivers/crypto/intel/qat/qat_common/
  adf_accel_devices.h. Mainline has no 4xxx VF driver (drivers/crypto/intel/qat/
  Makefile), so the VFs stay unbound until the plugin binds them to vfio-pci.
  virtfn<n> links on the PF: intel-device-plugins v0.37.1
  cmd/qat_plugin/dpdkdrv/dpdkdrv_test.go.
- allocatable qat.intel.com/cy: v0.37.1 cmd/qat_plugin/dpdkdrv/dpdkdrv.go maps
  cfg_services "sym;asym" (the value shown in Documentation/ABI/testing/
  sysfs-driver-qat) to "cy". dsa.intel.com/wq-user-dedicated: cmd/dsa_plugin/README.md.
- amx_*, rdt_a, cat_l3, cdp_l3, mba: arch/x86/include/asm/cpufeatures.h; ASPEED
  0x1a03:0x2000: pci.ids 2026.10.15.
- Xeon Platinum 8480+: 56 cores / 112 threads, 2S, QAT/DLB/DSA/IAA "1 default
  devices", Intel On Demand Activation Model "No", Intel RDT Yes:
  https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/231746/intel-xeon-platinum-8480-processor-105m-cache-2-00-ghz/specifications.html
  (one DSA and one IAA per socket matches the default; the single QAT PF leaves
  socket 1 below it).
- node<N>/cpulist, cpu<N>/topology/physical_package_id, cache/index3/shared_cpu_list,
  PCI numa_node: Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-devices-node,
  Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-devices-system-cpu, Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-bus-pci.
  SNC off: CPU 0's L3 list equals node0 (arch/x86/kernel/cpu/resctrl/monitor.c
  snc_get_config()).
- intel_vsec.sdsi.<n> under the OOBMSM function 0x09a7 bound to intel_vsec, one per
  socket: Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-driver-intel_sdsi,
  drivers/platform/x86/intel/vsec.c (PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_VSEC_OOBMSM, the
  "%s.%s.%d" auxiliary name). /sys/bus/auxiliary/devices/<name> as a relative link
  to the parent PCI device, as read on a live host (intel_vsec.telemetry.0).
- uncore_imc_<n>/events/cas_count_{read,write} with .scale 6.103515625e-5 and .unit
  MiB, printed with no trailing newline: spr_uncore_imc_events in
  arch/x86/events/intel/uncore_snbep.c, uncore_event_show() in uncore.c.

Layout: sysfs paths and relative symlinks follow the driver core's naming
(class devices under <pci dev>/<class>/<name>; /sys/class/<class>/<name> and
/sys/bus/pci/devices/<bdf> as relative links; <dev>/driver -> bus/pci/drivers/<drv>),
in exactly the form tests/fixtures/capture.sh writes (the matrix test re-captures
each tree and requires an identical result).

Not from hardware: BDFs, char-device minors, the kernel release string, the CPU
numbering (cores 0-111 by socket, SMT siblings +112), the OOBMSM class code, the
number of uncore_imc units (two of a socket's set), the brand string in model name
(built from the ARK processor number), and the cpuinfo flags line (a
representative subset in cpufeatures.h order).
